20:57.798 --> 21:01.825
And the idea is this flow between

21:02.181 --> 21:05.350
getting your order and getting the food out the door or out the window as

21:05.414 --> 21:08.316
quick as possible. So it's hard to,

21:08.961 --> 21:12.880
if you have customer seating, to rearrange that kitchen and your

21:13.380 --> 21:17.433
customer seating to put your front door and your drive-through

21:17.562 --> 21:23.818
window on the same space. So it's For

21:24.268 --> 21:27.902
for a bank, for a

21:28.288 --> 21:30.781
tire shop, for a loop shop,

21:31.407 --> 21:36.331
it's it's not a problem. But for food and multi-tenant

21:36.831 --> 21:38.074
buildings, it it's a problem.

21:39.668 --> 21:43.131
So we have we would like to be doing

21:43.406 --> 21:47.176
projects right here on this corner. We'd like to be doing things

21:47.694 --> 21:50.500
south of. Holiday Oil. We've,

21:50.613 --> 21:54.904
we've had tenants and we're kind of on

21:55.404 --> 21:59.109
the shelf right now with everything. So is the

21:59.962 --> 22:03.441
ask just for fast food restaurants to move

22:03.941 --> 22:07.832
it to the side versus having it in the back? My— I, I am not

22:07.881 --> 22:11.049
here to give any suggestions because I didn't, I didn't write the code.

22:11.194 --> 22:14.799
If I did give a suggestion, I would say just scrap what you— Altogether?

22:16.740 --> 22:18.857
altogether. Um, because coupled with that, again,

22:20.668 --> 22:23.934
you can't put these buildings right up on the property line,

22:25.486 --> 22:27.531
um, just with the traffic flow.

22:29.906 --> 22:33.092
So I'm not here to give you suggestions. I'm just telling— giving you

22:33.140 --> 22:36.262
some of the issues that we're facing right now with our

22:36.762 --> 22:40.843
tenants. I, I have a question for you, Mr. Gackle. I mean,

22:40.876 --> 22:44.351
and I'm just going to say this, I'm I don't know who's bent

22:44.399 --> 22:47.476
on this being this way. Let me just say it that way. I'm certainly not.

22:48.822 --> 22:52.268
And if we've got this wrong, then let's fix it. Yeah. That's my thought

22:52.768 --> 22:56.275
process. But we need someone like you to help us understand what the problems

22:56.775 --> 22:59.272
are that it's creating. And so my ask would be to have you work with

22:59.304 --> 23:02.285
our planning staff to make a suggestion as to how to correct it so it

23:02.785 --> 23:06.580
functions. From my perspective, the important part of this is the screening

23:06.612 --> 23:09.721
element. I just wanna make sure that those things are screened, even if

23:10.221 --> 23:13.580
there's a drive-through that's adjacent to one of our major roadways, I'd like to make

23:14.080 --> 23:18.431
sure that it's screened with some bushes or a berm or something that just kind

23:18.931 --> 23:22.413
of keeps that separation between the pedestrian traffic that may be walking along

23:22.526 --> 23:25.760
there from that drive-through aisle. So yeah,

23:26.568 --> 23:30.481
we do, we do landscaping, we do berms, small retaining

23:30.594 --> 23:33.691
walls. And I see this as a screening code, frankly.

23:33.999 --> 23:37.247
And so the impact of the drive-through is not something that I've

23:37.344 --> 23:40.870
really been aware of. So it's It's good to hear your input, and my encouragement

23:40.902 --> 23:44.962
would be to get the mayor to let you work with staff and figure

23:45.462 --> 23:49.100
it out. Bring us back something that works. I would agree,

23:49.133 --> 23:52.619
and I think for me the most important thing is what the architectural facade

23:53.119 --> 23:56.471
that faces that main road, that it looks nicer. And,

23:57.006 --> 24:00.115
uh, so I think that combined with, you know, the berming,

24:00.617 --> 24:03.872
the screening, I think that's really for me what it is. I don't really care

24:04.372 --> 24:06.831
where the drive-through is. And if there's a safety issue, I mean, that's something that's

24:07.331 --> 24:10.592
pretty significant. So, okay.

24:11.738 --> 24:15.334
So rather than scrap it, I think the— if I understand

24:15.834 --> 24:17.848
correctly, I want to make sure you understand that we're going to look at,

24:18.073 --> 24:20.667
uh, some berming requirements and, uh,

24:21.617 --> 24:24.931
the facade on the street-facing and

24:25.431 --> 24:28.451
maybe tweak that a little bit. Or some of that's already in our code,

24:28.467 --> 24:32.220
right? And maybe remove the location requirement because I think that that's part

24:32.720 --> 24:36.020
of what's giving you issues, right? I'm glad

24:36.520 --> 24:39.660
to work. Is that agreeable and understood? Yeah,

24:40.160 --> 24:44.100
absolutely. Well, we have a good working relationship with Bill, and we'll definitely

24:44.600 --> 24:47.380
work with him. Because, Bill, we're interested in attracting more business to the city,

24:47.880 --> 24:51.400
not less. And you're the guy that can help us understand how to do that.

24:51.900 --> 24:54.140
And if you're running into these problems, I'm really grateful that you're willing to bring

24:54.640 --> 24:57.280
it forward and let us understand that there's a problem with our code. One of

24:57.780 --> 25:23.674
the things that I

25:23.722 --> 25:26.433
think it would've looked a little more attractive. And that's, so that's what we were,

25:27.315 --> 25:30.587
we just trying to make the city look, you know, make it look nice.

25:30.763 --> 25:34.003
And so I, we'd love to work with

25:34.052 --> 25:37.629
Bill to make it, to make that screening requirement as best

25:37.693 --> 25:40.933
as possible. We thought maybe like some type of awning or some,

25:41.206 --> 25:44.798
something, I don't know, some, I don't know, I mean, just brainstorming. I think

25:45.298 --> 25:48.408
that's what Council Member Whiting is talking about is just, you know, still try to

25:48.908 --> 25:51.793
enhance the front facade even if it has a drive-through on it. Yeah,

25:52.438 --> 25:55.645
yeah. I think some of the code includes that.

25:56.548 --> 25:59.611
Yeah, that's— I think it's in other parts of the code. Yeah,

26:00.152 --> 26:03.615
there's— and there's berming also requirements or screening requirements,

26:03.631 --> 26:06.317
especially against residential, but this will be on a street,

26:07.288 --> 26:10.395
so incorporate that. And I think we'll— You guys iron it out and bring it

26:10.427 --> 26:14.148
back. Yeah, we will. Bill's good to work with. We'll figure

26:14.648 --> 26:17.422
it out. Okay. Okay, thank you. Anything else on that one?

26:18.212 --> 26:21.082
If I can have a little leeway, I wanted to go out of order a

26:21.582 --> 26:25.121
little bit because I wasn't paying attention. But if we can skip to item 13A,

26:25.169 --> 26:28.968
Lead the way. Specifically because I

26:29.468 --> 26:32.206
know I've been up at the state capitol today and I know people are busy

26:32.719 --> 26:36.406
and I wasn't paying attention to who was here. So this

26:36.906 --> 26:39.211
should be quick, but I wanted to jump over. It's a resolution of Eagle Mountain

26:39.711 --> 26:43.267
City. It's on the consent agenda, approving agreement with Hamco Consulting

26:43.299 --> 26:46.216
to provide lobbying services. with Mr. Dan Hammert.

26:46.360 --> 26:50.414
So if you want to come forward, it's really questions

26:50.914 --> 26:54.292
and kind of a meet and greet, if you will, or whatever. Make sure the

26:54.792 --> 26:58.153
mic is turned on. There's a little button. Okay, we're good. Mr.

26:58.202 --> 27:01.535
Dan. Thank you, and happy to wait as long

27:01.567 --> 27:04.323
as it takes. So, um, uh,

27:05.396 --> 27:08.409
yeah, so, so nice to meet y'all. I've met some of you in meetings before,

27:08.841 --> 27:11.790
uh, on other issues, uh, specifically about some,

27:12.127 --> 27:15.337
uh, uranium enrichment stuff, some, uh,

27:15.353 --> 27:19.012
nuclear stuff, and some, uh, Military Installation Development Authority

27:19.205 --> 27:22.334
activity. But regardless, uh, here before you, um,

27:22.350 --> 27:24.565
I'll give you a little bit about my background if that's okay,

27:25.207 --> 27:28.754
and then talk about some of the projects that, uh,

27:29.059 --> 27:32.092
hoping to work on for your city this legislative session.

27:32.830 --> 27:36.040
And then also, you have already a lobbyist, Dave Stewart,

27:36.056 --> 27:39.843
and just thought it might be helpful to talk about how I would work with

27:40.343 --> 27:43.280
So, first of all, about me. I'm relatively new to lobbying. This is my 4th

27:43.345 --> 27:47.295
session, but I'm not new to state government. I served in the state legislature,

27:47.311 --> 27:51.293
in the Senate. I represented Northeast Utah County, where it's

27:51.793 --> 27:55.436
the seat that Brady Brammer is now sitting in. Served there, was the

27:55.644 --> 28:00.044
majority whip in the Senate, and part of the still current

28:00.544 --> 28:02.469
Senate president's leadership team at the time.

28:03.817 --> 28:07.526
When Governor Cox was elected governor, he asked me if I would resign my Senate

28:08.026 --> 28:10.411
seat. I'd just been reelected. I was going to say, and join his cabinet and

28:10.444 --> 28:13.856
run what was then the Governor's Office of Economic Development.

28:14.794 --> 28:17.932
That seemed like an interesting, unique opportunity, so I said

28:17.965 --> 28:22.160
yes to that. I resigned from the Senate and then ran GOED.

28:23.015 --> 28:26.758
I did that for a few years. We changed the name to GOEO, and ironically,

28:26.823 --> 28:30.484
they're changing the name back to GOED this session. I guess

28:30.984 --> 28:34.898
the name didn't stick. Jeff Moss, who didn't

28:35.398 --> 28:39.018
represent this area but was right next to it, is now He was asked

28:39.050 --> 28:42.740
by the governor to resign from the House and head

28:43.240 --> 28:46.222
up that agency, which he's decided to do. So I've had a lot of experience

28:46.351 --> 28:49.656
in state legislature. As a

28:50.156 --> 28:53.058
senator, did quite a bit with transportation, and that's going to be germane to what

28:53.558 --> 28:57.134
I'm hoping to work on with you guys about working with UDOT,

28:57.840 --> 29:01.482
Carlos Braceras and his leadership team specifically. And then

29:01.594 --> 29:05.125
since leaving the legislature, have found there's not a lot of lobbyists that

29:05.625 --> 29:09.386
really specialize in this area. And so given my

29:09.886 --> 29:12.698
relationship with Carlos, I did serve on the cabinet with him and knew him from

29:13.198 --> 29:17.232
before that as a legislator, do have a really good working

29:17.732 --> 29:21.170
relationship with him and his senior staff. And so that's been an area

29:21.670 --> 29:24.855
where I've been hired by a number of groups to help

29:24.871 --> 29:28.052
with funding, transportation-type funding.

29:28.888 --> 29:32.310
Which takes me specifically to what I'm hoping

29:32.810 --> 29:34.936
to work on with you, and I have some notes here I'm gonna refer to,

29:34.953 --> 29:38.809
but mostly it's— Yeah. 'Cause I get nervous and forget

29:39.309 --> 29:42.970
what I'm about to say. But mostly

29:43.470 --> 29:47.010
what I've been asked to work on is the Mid-Valley Highway

29:47.510 --> 29:50.838
specifically. You guys have major transportation issues. There was a—

29:51.017 --> 29:54.238
there was— geez, you know this— going east-west in and out of

29:54.659 --> 29:58.882
this area is a nightmare. I actually— this

29:58.995 --> 30:02.297
is dumb, but I'll still say it— before— or I still own

30:02.394 --> 30:05.536
Red Hanger Cleaners. It's a little dry cleaning company. We actually have a store

30:06.049 --> 30:09.895
in Saratoga Springs. And so I've come

30:10.395 --> 30:14.111
out here. We offer a pickup delivery service, which I've had the fortune or misfortune

30:14.143 --> 30:16.948
of driving those vans around when someone doesn't show up for work.

30:17.669 --> 30:20.971
And I've driven all over this area. And traffic here is not great,

30:21.372 --> 30:25.475
particularly coming out to this area specifically.

30:25.587 --> 30:28.777
Like even today, I came early because I was worried I was going

30:29.277 --> 30:32.399
to be late. And coming off of Redwood,

30:32.672 --> 30:35.714
to turn, to come out into this valley.

30:36.313 --> 30:39.808
I think there was a stackup of cars to turn left there. I think

30:40.308 --> 30:43.585
it was like a quarter of a mile. This was at 3, like 3 PM.

30:43.762 --> 30:47.095
It's crazy. Of course. Yeah. And I know you,

30:47.208 --> 30:51.121
I know I'm preaching to the choir here, but that's the big, I guess

30:51.233 --> 30:54.964
what we're trying to help you with is get more connection access

30:55.640 --> 30:58.599
in and out of this area and specifically the Mid-Valley Highway.

30:59.138 --> 31:03.129
So the big lifts, and I'm actually quite optimistic

31:03.629 --> 31:06.704
already. We've already been working on this. So whether you hire me or not,

31:06.736 --> 31:09.205
it'll probably get done. So I shouldn't have said that. You probably don't need to

31:09.705 --> 31:12.587
hire me. But we've laid a lot of groundwork here.

31:13.052 --> 31:16.595
But what we're trying to do is twofold. One, get the

31:17.095 --> 31:20.041
plans for the Mid-Valley Highway designated as a state road. It's not.

31:20.683 --> 31:24.770
The whole concept's not. So that's number one. And in conjunction of

31:25.270 --> 31:29.164
that, to start UDOT to begin

31:29.277 --> 31:32.498
working on this as a major infrastructure project, because also

31:32.998 --> 31:36.618
it's not. And so what we're working on is an environmental study to get money

31:36.779 --> 31:40.237
set aside for an environmental study to start

31:40.285 --> 31:43.933
moving the project forward. And right

31:44.433 --> 31:48.912
now I'm actually— this is a pretty— how

31:48.961 --> 31:52.610
do I say this in a public meeting? It's like it's hard

31:53.110 --> 31:55.416
to do this. Yeah. And it's hard to get this done in the way we're

31:55.916 --> 31:59.872
doing it. But right now, I'm pretty optimistic. You have an amazing representative

32:00.209 --> 32:03.559
in Stephanie Griecious. She is a bulldog in all the right ways.

32:04.376 --> 32:08.176
And she's great to work with. She's tenacious, and she's

32:08.224 --> 32:12.295
hardworking, and she's been super helpful in this process.

32:13.770 --> 32:17.569
And I think we are gonna be successful this session on

32:17.617 --> 32:21.545
those 2 points. The other areas where I'm working is Cedar Valley Highway. It's another

32:22.045 --> 32:26.324
highway planned, right? Yes. On some specifically, corridor preservation,

32:26.372 --> 32:29.165
and then ideally to get some money to build the first part of the frontage

32:29.665 --> 32:32.986
road on that highway. And that highway's planned, as you may know, 2 frontage roads

32:33.002 --> 32:36.518
with the main highway in the middle. And so start that process.

32:37.915 --> 32:41.190
Some other areas that we have worked

32:41.690 --> 32:45.091
on that have been brought to our attention, Senator Scott Sandel has a bill that

32:45.139 --> 32:48.720
prohibits any sort of incentive and then redistributes any

32:48.816 --> 32:52.894
tax increment coming from data centers. I know that's something

32:53.006 --> 32:56.540
that this community has really focused on as a benefit

32:57.040 --> 33:00.590
to the community. That is a bill that I have engaged in with Senator Sandel.

33:00.638 --> 33:03.353
I think that's going to end up in a fine place. He mostly just put

33:03.853 --> 33:07.588
it out there because he wants to start a discussion, which many

33:08.088 --> 33:11.762
legislators do it this way. His concern is there are going to be some haves

33:12.262 --> 33:16.178
and have-nots relative to some of these larger infrastructure

33:16.678 --> 33:19.833
projects. The haves are going to benefit, and the have-nots are not.

33:19.882 --> 33:23.477
Where he represents a pretty rural area, It's most

33:23.977 --> 33:28.060
of northwest Utah. He's concerned

33:28.560 --> 33:31.345
that his communities, or the communities he represents, are going to be have-nots.

33:31.746 --> 33:35.047
And so he doesn't want to find himself in a

33:35.547 --> 33:38.828
situation where some communities benefit. It's not targeted at Eagle Mountain,

33:38.956 --> 33:42.642
but that is his concern. And I think we'll have a dialogue and a discussion

33:43.142 --> 33:46.712
around that. And then also, I've been

33:47.212 --> 33:50.621
working on a broader UDOT study. that kind of brings

33:51.121 --> 33:55.059
into play all of these connectors that come in and around this valley to

33:55.075 --> 33:57.974
help figure out how to get people in and out of this valley better.

33:58.022 --> 34:01.670
So that is a completely separate kind of process and study that UDOT

34:02.170 --> 34:05.318
is working on or should start working on here in the very near

34:05.383 --> 34:08.611
term. That's— oh, David Stewart. How do I

34:08.660 --> 34:12.698
work with him? I've been brought

34:13.198 --> 34:15.712
on with David on a number of projects. Not that he's deficient. If I was

34:16.212 --> 34:19.137
going to hire a lobbyist, I'd hire David. So, you know, you hired the right

34:19.169 --> 34:22.622
guy. But what I found is different people

34:23.122 --> 34:26.637
just have different relationships and frankly different ideas to

34:27.137 --> 34:31.295
approach situations. And where David and I have worked

34:31.795 --> 34:36.724
together well and we've been hired as a team by a few cities

34:36.756 --> 34:40.434
and even a few counties is different

34:40.934 --> 34:44.192
people have different relationships, but also different people, like I said, have different ideas on

34:44.289 --> 34:48.556
how to approach a project and a process. And specifically to the

34:49.438 --> 34:52.452
Mid-Valley Highway, I happened to be in a meeting,

34:53.270 --> 34:57.454
this was on the other side of the mountains in American Fork, where Senator

34:57.954 --> 35:01.862
Brammer is trying to get some money for a flyover

35:02.362 --> 35:06.127
that comes over the freeway to connect into the FrontRunner station there in American

35:06.627 --> 35:10.135
Fork. And he got talking to me and he

35:10.600 --> 35:13.726
wasn't— he was asking for some advice on how to go about doing it.

35:13.806 --> 35:17.238
And we set up a meeting with UDOT and him and MAG to discuss,

35:17.254 --> 35:21.541
and American Fork, how to move this forward. It was in that meeting where

35:22.295 --> 35:25.506
UDOT asked, they said they would help with it,

35:25.522 --> 35:29.488
but they asked for Senator Brammer to push for some intent language

35:29.536 --> 35:33.005
to get an environmental study started on that project.

35:33.470 --> 35:36.023
It was in that moment and in that meeting where I said, hey, we should

35:36.523 --> 35:39.411
do the same thing for Eagle Mountain City. It's kind of being in the right

35:39.911 --> 35:42.946
place at the right time, but having also different ideas and expertise on how to

35:43.446 --> 35:46.221
approach a problem and get stuff done. And I've,

35:46.560 --> 35:49.883
in the 4 years I've been out of the legislature and on a number of

35:50.383 --> 35:53.774
these projects working with David, I found that we complement each other very well.

35:54.550 --> 35:58.268
And that comes back to, I'm gonna repeat myself

35:58.284 --> 36:01.889
now, but just different relationships and just different ideas

36:01.921 --> 36:05.094
on how to solve a problem. So hopefully that answers your questions. Happy to answer

36:05.143 --> 36:06.399
any questions you may have. Thank you.

36:09.338 --> 36:13.249
Could you just maybe educate us a little bit? How many other

36:13.281 --> 36:17.256
cities are you working with? And I'm more, as I was reading

36:17.272 --> 36:21.054
through this, just thinking about time allocation. You know, there's a specific ask

36:21.554 --> 36:25.141
on a dollar amount, and you don't have to get into like your hourly fee

36:25.641 --> 36:29.725
or anything like that, but I'd be curious just what your bandwidth is and how

36:29.789 --> 36:32.931
much time you feel like you'd be able to allocate to Eagle Mountain.

36:32.995 --> 36:35.976
Because you addressed some really key issues getting in and getting out.

36:36.040 --> 36:39.620
Mid-Valley is So important, we want to get it right and get it prioritized

36:39.733 --> 36:43.121
with the state. It's a great question. On the one

36:43.185 --> 36:46.236
hand, I feel like I always use

36:46.736 --> 36:50.123
this analogy of balls rolling down hills, right? You kick them down the hill and

36:50.171 --> 36:53.286
you watch them roll. We've kicked a lot of those balls down the hill already

36:53.786 --> 36:56.113
and they're rolling quite well. I mean, it still takes some work and effort,

36:56.177 --> 36:59.983
but as far as bandwidth for this session, on the items I discussed, I'm pretty

37:00.031 --> 37:04.543
optimistic on getting those things done this session or significant

37:05.043 --> 37:08.054
progress. Well, the way I described them, actually getting them done. Nothing's done until it's

37:08.554 --> 37:11.830
done, but I'm pretty optimistic. On the other hand, just general bandwidth,

37:12.266 --> 37:15.838
I am busy. And I'll even tell you the other cities I do work

37:16.338 --> 37:19.121
for. So Santaquin, Perry,

37:20.270 --> 37:23.892
Tremonton. So they're kind of far away. And this is where this matters.

37:24.070 --> 37:29.145
I also represent Salt Lake City. This matters because geographically,

37:31.820 --> 37:34.449
UDOT and the state likes to spread the love. Yeah. I know there is a

37:34.949 --> 37:37.853
narrative that for the past few years a lot more transportation money's been going north

37:38.126 --> 37:40.920
than south. I don't know if you've heard that narrative, but it's been out there

37:41.420 --> 37:45.287
for a while. Regardless, and there are some large projects

37:45.335 --> 37:49.430
to the north that are getting funded, but traditionally the state

37:49.446 --> 37:52.866
likes to, again, spread the love or

37:52.978 --> 37:57.555
share dollars based on need and geographically.

37:58.036 --> 38:01.342
And so to that extent, there's not

38:01.842 --> 38:05.062
as much as a conflict with the cities I do represent with this area because

38:05.238 --> 38:09.552
of that desire by UDOT to spread out resources

38:10.289 --> 38:14.074
and not put them all in one place. And so while I

38:14.574 --> 38:17.088
am busy and while I do have other clients, I don't know that they're in

38:17.120 --> 38:20.536
direct conflict. I don't know if that answers your question exactly, but— Yeah,

38:20.552 --> 38:24.128
or more just like out of a 40-hour work week, you know, throughout the year,

38:24.320 --> 38:28.097
or do all— is all the work happening For most

38:28.597 --> 38:31.996
of it, it happens during the session. During the work week? Yeah. So I spend,

38:33.427 --> 38:36.970
I bet it's 16, 17-hour days during the session,

38:37.521 --> 38:40.967
during the work week. On weekends, you're still working. It's not

38:41.467 --> 38:45.133
that much though. I'm gonna guess like last weekend, probably 3 to 4 hours

38:45.230 --> 38:48.134
on Monday and probably 4 to 5 hours on Saturday.

38:48.779 --> 38:51.925
And so the session is definitely the busy time.

38:52.367 --> 38:55.505
For anyone who's a CPA or interacts with CPAs, it's kind of like around April

38:55.537 --> 38:58.498
15th for those folks. That's your busy season.

38:59.246 --> 39:02.700
The rest of the year you are engaged though. I thought

39:03.217 --> 39:05.444
lobbyists only worked during the session.

39:06.025 --> 39:09.220
And what I like to say is either I'm doing it way wrong or I

39:09.252 --> 39:12.461
was wrong because I'm pretty busy year round. So for

39:12.961 --> 39:14.687
example, on your projects, let's say we get them funded.

39:16.717 --> 39:20.311
Those bills are all gonna pass by the end of the first week of

39:20.811 --> 39:23.313
March. Yeah. Right? And they're gonna have effective dates in May. But as soon as

39:23.813 --> 39:27.398
they're done, I know what I'm gonna go do. I'm gonna go start talking

39:27.430 --> 39:31.361
to UDOT about, okay, we got you this

39:31.861 --> 39:35.564
money, how do we get this allocated per the Transportation Commission?

39:35.612 --> 39:38.942
There's other boxes that have to be checked or

39:38.991 --> 39:42.950
hoops that have to be jumped through. And while things do

39:43.450 --> 39:47.035
slow down outside of the session, it's still, I've found at least,

39:47.051 --> 39:50.777
Yeah. 40-hour work weeks and still around

39:51.277 --> 39:55.446
and available. And also what I found is if a

39:55.494 --> 39:59.521
city has issues, I'm absolutely available to go work on them. And particularly

40:00.021 --> 40:04.046
if we're thinking ahead to the next session, that interim period

40:04.624 --> 40:08.779
between the sessions is a very effective time to get ahead on projects

40:09.116 --> 40:12.983
and to get ahead on whether it's funding requests for

40:13.483 --> 40:17.137
transportation or even policy initiatives. I'm making stuff up right now. But let's say

40:17.637 --> 40:21.207
you guys want to modify some stuff you're doing around your energy overlays

40:21.239 --> 40:25.277
or your, you know, your data center stuff you're doing, and you want some state

40:25.325 --> 40:28.914
policy to help support that or modify that.

40:29.491 --> 40:32.823
That interim period is an excellent time, and spending quite a bit of time,

40:33.256 --> 40:37.951
not for you per se, but it can be, but for me, with legislators

40:38.624 --> 40:42.021
getting language drafted ready for the coming session.

40:42.614 --> 40:46.175
So I do think I am around and available

40:46.577 --> 40:50.446
during the session and try to be responsive, but then post-session I'm around and available

40:50.495 --> 40:53.834
to work on issues. And I still go to work post-session

40:54.894 --> 40:59.037
as the follow-up to what happened in the session, but usually the orientation

40:59.647 --> 41:03.260
