01:29:14.665 --> 01:29:18.136
Do we want those connections in place? Because I've heard from some people in

01:29:18.636 --> 01:29:21.939
that area of great concerns with the elementary schools that are right there.

01:29:23.683 --> 01:29:27.089
And yeah. I would say that's exactly why

01:29:27.250 --> 01:29:31.047
we want to spend the money on it, because that is the impetus for maybe

01:29:31.192 --> 01:29:34.512
a pedestrian tunnel or changing a light so

01:29:35.012 --> 01:29:38.361
that it's a better intersection for safety. Absolutely. Or, you know, any of those things.

01:29:38.425 --> 01:29:42.114
And this is just arming us with data so that we can make

01:29:42.274 --> 01:29:45.883
good decisions about those areas. They have been areas

01:29:46.383 --> 01:29:49.652
of concern that residents have brought to us. And I think that's why we as

01:29:50.152 --> 01:29:52.924
a council said, and this was last year, just to give more context for you,

01:29:53.421 --> 01:29:56.757
we said, as long as we're doing these traffic studies and we're doing our transportation

01:29:56.789 --> 01:30:00.209
master plan, Can we please look at these specific areas

01:30:00.241 --> 01:30:04.223
that we receive a lot of emails and questions about? So I

01:30:04.239 --> 01:30:07.290
hope that helps clear it up. Okay, yeah, that sounds good. I, I did— I

01:30:07.790 --> 01:30:10.694
think it's something we need to be open to considering not connecting. I think,

01:30:10.742 --> 01:30:13.616
I think we need to look at all the possibilities here and,

01:30:13.712 --> 01:30:16.506
and do, I think, what's safest for the residents,

01:30:17.180 --> 01:30:20.600
especially. I mean, I went down there, um, at this location

01:30:20.793 --> 01:30:23.828
and watched kids running across the street At

01:30:24.328 --> 01:30:26.798
places they shouldn't be running across the street. And I've driven,

01:30:28.018 --> 01:30:31.614
you know, when I've driven and shown homes in Silver Lake when school's

01:30:32.114 --> 01:30:34.889
getting out, it's mayhem going down those roads.

01:30:35.548 --> 01:30:40.203
And if these suddenly connect into a high school, they're all— instead

01:30:40.703 --> 01:30:43.800
of channeling, you know, traffic down our bigger corridors,

01:30:43.992 --> 01:30:47.235
we're starting to channel them through neighborhoods. It's going to be problematic.

01:30:47.861 --> 01:30:51.866
Agreed. And I live near one of the schools in question off

01:30:52.366 --> 01:30:55.892
of Golden Eagle, Brookhaven. That's our neighborhood elementary school, so I'm very

01:30:56.392 --> 01:30:59.517
aware of the little kids and big kids, frankly, that cross there.

01:31:00.066 --> 01:31:04.284
I think this will help us make our best decisions about what

01:31:04.784 --> 01:31:05.480
we connect and what we don't.

01:31:08.568 --> 01:31:11.916
It'll also— it also— I mean, just to speak to that, if you'll

01:31:11.932 --> 01:31:15.893
allow me, Council Member Whiting, it also will help us understand the

01:31:15.974 --> 01:31:19.760
impact of making some of those connections, the expense

01:31:20.690 --> 01:31:24.076
of the additional infrastructure that's going to be required if we

01:31:24.156 --> 01:31:27.413
do or if we don't make those connections. Because if that connection is

01:31:27.445 --> 01:31:30.862
made, it may precipitate us needing to put some signals into some residential

01:31:30.894 --> 01:31:34.264
neighborhoods, traffic signals. They don't

01:31:34.764 --> 01:31:37.665
give those away anymore. They never did, but they're getting more and more expensive.

01:31:37.713 --> 01:31:40.874
So I think that's part of the reason why we want to look at what

01:31:40.989 --> 01:31:44.142
the data so that we can make a good informed decision about

01:31:44.256 --> 01:31:45.269
how to move forward there.

