02:50:54.372 --> 02:50:57.570
request that has been submitted in here is to ask for

02:50:57.586 --> 02:51:01.330
an additional $500,000 between now and June 30th.

02:51:01.908 --> 02:51:05.957
That would, on average, allow us to issue about, you know, 4 grants

02:51:06.457 --> 02:51:09.894
a month through the rest of the fiscal year. So— Can you give

02:51:10.394 --> 02:51:13.687
us an idea? I know that it's kind of a moving target because some

02:51:13.719 --> 02:51:17.368
of our projects are coming online and haven't

02:51:17.868 --> 02:51:22.013
reached their full property value potential. But what are we typically bringing

02:51:22.142 --> 02:51:25.420
in every year into the fund right now?

02:51:25.533 --> 02:51:28.913
What was last year's? It's about $1.5 million. So last

02:51:29.010 --> 02:51:32.859
year was a bigger influx because we had some more area that triggered from

02:51:32.907 --> 02:51:38.144
that, but about $1.5 million right now per

02:51:38.644 --> 02:51:41.494
year. We had a balance going into it. Yes.

02:51:41.815 --> 02:51:45.749
So I have all of that information available if you want me to

02:51:46.249 --> 02:51:49.763
pull it up. It's in my notes, but I can't see my notes

02:51:50.263 --> 02:51:51.946
and I can't tell you all the numbers off the top of my head.

02:51:52.027 --> 02:51:55.383
It's okay. But we have reserves, so it doesn't impact the reserves. It really

02:51:55.883 --> 02:51:58.209
isn't going to impact your ability to do any of the other programs. So that

02:51:58.709 --> 02:52:01.291
was one of the things we wanted to talk to with Kiara and Evan,

02:52:01.324 --> 02:52:04.760
you know, like with the housing programs and the different things. I know you guys

02:52:05.260 --> 02:52:08.629
have had some information that's come through through from the planning side, and there's

02:52:09.129 --> 02:52:11.246
just a lot of things that we're looking at. And that was something that was

02:52:11.343 --> 02:52:14.474
important to the council, is that we don't want to tie up all of the

02:52:14.974 --> 02:52:19.114
affordable housing dollars into a single program. We want to have diversity

02:52:19.387 --> 02:52:23.225
in terms of what we're able to do with these funds and available.

02:52:23.883 --> 02:52:27.415
One other thing that we are using this money for is we do

02:52:27.915 --> 02:52:31.687
use it to reimburse the general fund for our mandatory contribution to the homeless

02:52:32.187 --> 02:52:36.086
shelter. So the state takes a percentage of our sales tax

02:52:36.586 --> 02:52:40.164
dollars. It's based on a population base each month that goes there. So the

02:52:40.664 --> 02:52:44.242
reimbursement amount right now is about $235,000

02:52:44.291 --> 02:52:47.791
a year. And that money comes out of affordable

02:52:48.291 --> 02:52:51.628
housing and it goes and it reimburses the general fund from those sales tax dollars

02:52:51.741 --> 02:52:55.498
that were used for that contribution initially. So it's a qualifying event and

02:52:55.819 --> 02:52:59.379
we— and so we use that for that purpose. Is the Olin-Walker

02:52:59.879 --> 02:53:03.245
contribution based on population? It is based on population,

02:53:03.261 --> 02:53:06.722
so it changes every year in January, and then we get

02:53:07.222 --> 02:53:11.137
our number and we're set for the year. So I think it's

02:53:11.185 --> 02:53:13.837
important for the new council members to understand that we're going to pay that one

02:53:13.934 --> 02:53:17.072
way or the other. It'll come out of general fund money

02:53:17.121 --> 02:53:21.456
if it has to, because it's a contribution that the state— it's a mandatory contribution

02:53:21.956 --> 02:53:25.067
to that fund. And because we have these RDA dollars that come in through the

02:53:25.567 --> 02:53:29.052
affordable housing, it's been able to fund that contribution. Sure.

02:53:29.293 --> 02:53:32.745
So it's really helpful that that happened. But, but it— there,

02:53:32.890 --> 02:53:37.353
we're generating $1.5 million, and that number is going to go up a year in

02:53:37.610 --> 02:53:41.255
RDA affordable housing dollars, at least for the foreseeable future.

02:53:41.319 --> 02:53:44.450
That looks like that's going to be the case. So I'll just say

02:53:44.950 --> 02:53:48.432
from my perspective, I have no issues allocating an additional $500,000

02:53:48.448 --> 02:53:52.524
from my perspective, Kimberly, to that, to that process through the end of fiscal

02:53:52.941 --> 02:53:56.501
2020— is it 2025, 2026? I have no

02:53:57.001 --> 02:54:00.895
issues with that. I don't know how everyone else feels about it. And I

02:54:01.395 --> 02:54:04.391
think it's really important. One of the things that's made me really nervous about these

02:54:04.488 --> 02:54:08.401
funds is that they continue to grow, and we really had no program in

02:54:08.433 --> 02:54:12.330
place where we could expend them. And my concern was

02:54:12.586 --> 02:54:15.537
that big brother was going to look over our shoulder and say, gosh, Eagle Mountain,

02:54:15.585 --> 02:54:18.812
if you've got $2.5 million or $3.5 million or $5 million for

02:54:19.312 --> 02:54:22.420
affordable housing that you're not putting to work, we've got a good place for it

02:54:22.920 --> 02:54:25.608
to go, and it's going to be in Draper or somewhere else. I don't know.

02:54:26.720 --> 02:54:30.443
And so I think it's really important that we're cognizant of putting these funds

02:54:30.507 --> 02:54:33.569
to work to produce exactly what they're designed to produce.

02:54:34.958 --> 02:54:38.370
I wholeheartedly agree with Council Member Wright. I think the thing that

02:54:38.870 --> 02:54:42.866
I would ask though is, um, is staff starting

02:54:42.882 --> 02:54:46.457
to look at potential other options? And will

02:54:46.570 --> 02:54:49.574
that be something that we'll get to talk about soon as a council?

02:54:50.040 --> 02:54:53.173
Because we've talked about revitalization grants,

02:54:53.189 --> 02:54:57.122
we've talked about ADU grants, different things like that, and we

02:54:57.622 --> 02:55:00.519
never wanted it to be like a voucher system per se.

02:55:01.150 --> 02:55:05.495
We always wanted to foster homeownership and just stability

02:55:05.608 --> 02:55:09.045
within our community. And I don't want it all to

02:55:09.077 --> 02:55:12.786
go to MAP, but right now that's the only program we have.

02:55:13.022 --> 02:55:16.843
So as we're looking to the future, I'm excited

02:55:17.421 --> 02:55:20.809
for, especially with some of our new council members and their experience,

02:55:21.114 --> 02:55:24.470
looking at other ways that we can make good use of this money.

02:55:25.064 --> 02:55:27.714
It sounds like the demand is still pretty high for the program, right?

02:55:28.838 --> 02:55:32.226
We're not having any trouble placing those resources in the hands of

02:55:32.290 --> 02:55:35.822
people who qualify? That's correct. What we are seeing is we

02:55:35.887 --> 02:55:39.258
are issuing more Tier 3 grants than we are the Tier

02:55:39.274 --> 02:55:42.598
1 and Tier 2. And so one of the things that

02:55:42.646 --> 02:55:45.922
we might talk about is that even though

02:55:46.422 --> 02:55:49.535
we have a certain amount of dollar that is there, do you want it to

02:55:50.035 --> 02:55:53.019
be broke out between those tiers where we're trying to target, or is it just

02:55:53.067 --> 02:55:56.632
a matter— as long as the goal is that we can help people get

02:55:56.696 --> 02:56:00.132
to Eagle Mountain? So I would love to see the data

02:56:00.228 --> 02:56:04.403
on how that's been distributed, Kimberly. I know it would fund about 40 forgivable loans,

02:56:05.286 --> 02:56:08.674
um, the million dollars that was set aside for those, those purposes.

02:56:09.461 --> 02:56:12.078
I would be really interested in just seeing that data. How long is it taking

02:56:12.174 --> 02:56:15.706
for those loans, those applications to be made? How many

02:56:16.206 --> 02:56:18.950
are we seeing a month on average? How many are approved? How many are rejected?

02:56:19.480 --> 02:56:22.964
How many qualify? How many don't? It'd just be good to get a sense of,

02:56:23.092 --> 02:56:26.898
is this something that's going to continue in perpetuity, at least

02:56:27.398 --> 02:56:31.859
to some degree? Is there going to be a waning a

02:56:32.359 --> 02:56:36.210
waning desire to participate or need to participate? And if so, I think we'd like

02:56:36.710 --> 02:56:40.224
to be aware of that sooner rather than later. So we are planning for

02:56:40.289 --> 02:56:43.934
a retreat in April. We're talking about having a retreat maybe even before

02:56:44.078 --> 02:56:47.225
that time. We're trying to find a date, and that's one of the things I

02:56:47.257 --> 02:56:51.207
wanted to talk to the council with later tonight. But with that,

02:56:51.287 --> 02:56:55.029
one of the topics is specifically RDA funds, how you want to

02:56:55.045 --> 02:56:58.230
use them. and prioritize them. That came out of the previous retreat as something

02:56:58.730 --> 02:57:00.068
that you wanted to come back to discuss.

02:57:06.410 --> 02:57:10.469
That's it. That's it. So— So

02:57:10.969 --> 02:57:13.865
is there any other questions about the presentation? This is a public hearing, so— You

02:57:14.365 --> 02:57:17.441
can ask all the questions. And I want to get them out before the public

02:57:17.941 --> 02:57:21.330
hearing. That's actually a good thing. So— Anything else?

02:57:22.261 --> 02:57:25.136
Okay, thank you, Kimberly. With that, this is a public hearing,

02:57:26.598 --> 02:57:29.891
and so we will take care of that now, and I

02:57:30.391 --> 02:57:33.810
will open the public hearing at 8:31. And before I do that,

02:57:33.826 --> 02:57:37.167
actually, Lacey, is our recording good, the streaming and everything?

02:57:37.296 --> 02:57:39.657
Okay, sorry, I'm a little late on that. I tried.

02:57:41.055 --> 02:57:44.845
Okay, so at 8:31 we'll open the public hearing. Is there anyone here to

02:57:45.345 --> 02:57:52.868
speak on that? Yeah, Jesse Southworth.

02:57:52.981 --> 02:57:56.747
Hello, excuse me, Mayor and Council. Um, I'm sure if you saw my face during

02:57:56.780 --> 02:58:00.013
that, you know that I'm not super thrilled with

02:58:00.513 --> 02:58:03.252
the budget. Um, I first had an issue just with the,

02:58:04.291 --> 02:58:07.940
the digital sign at Quarry Ride Park because I think that our current

02:58:07.972 --> 02:58:10.165
signs are, uh,

02:58:11.599 --> 02:58:15.346
semi-worthless. Mostly they're hard

02:58:15.846 --> 02:58:18.877
to see and they're primarily used to promote the city's social media pages.

02:58:19.623 --> 02:58:22.976
Sometimes I see videos of fields on there, but I'd prefer to look at the

02:58:23.476 --> 02:58:26.897
real thing. So I think that the signs are

02:58:27.397 --> 02:58:30.333
a waste of money, but at first I was thinking that it was going to

02:58:30.382 --> 02:58:33.576
be something where, oh, we have this extra money, let's put it on these signs,

02:58:34.883 --> 02:58:38.788
as Zach asked. But it

02:58:39.288 --> 02:58:41.483
seems that we have extra money, so now I'd like to know what is it

02:58:41.499 --> 02:58:44.642
that we have to do to get some of that $5 million that

02:58:45.142 --> 02:58:48.845
we have sitting around invested into some of the older parts of our city.

02:58:49.262 --> 02:58:53.145
Um, City Center right here, you just go east of here, there are

02:58:53.690 --> 02:58:56.867
plenty of trails and things that are— there's no

02:58:56.979 --> 02:59:00.332
paved paths, things that were left unfinished. And repeatedly last

02:59:00.832 --> 02:59:03.974
year I heard that we weren't going to do anything with this open space

02:59:04.474 --> 02:59:07.006
because we don't have any money to do it. And now that seems that— I

02:59:07.506 --> 02:59:09.429
don't know, either we're going to put a sign and use the money there,

02:59:09.557 --> 02:59:13.102
or we have extra money sitting somewhere that could be done there. So now I'm

02:59:13.602 --> 02:59:17.161
just curious as to what could be done, um,

02:59:17.514 --> 02:59:20.931
to get that money used there. Also, I know that it's

02:59:20.963 --> 02:59:24.300
probably $2,000 maybe a year that to maintain some

02:59:24.348 --> 02:59:27.861
of the CMS programs and the, the software for the signs, but I

02:59:28.361 --> 02:59:32.129
do know that those digital signs, especially one that would be the

02:59:32.629 --> 02:59:35.883
size that I expect, the one at the park

02:59:36.383 --> 02:59:38.163
would be, cost significantly more.

02:59:39.560 --> 02:59:43.349
Usually it's about 10 to 15% of the

02:59:43.670 --> 02:59:46.882
initial cost of installing it per year just

02:59:47.382 --> 02:59:51.024
to maintain functionality of it. But I mean, I'm willing to

02:59:51.056 --> 02:59:54.268
be wrong on that, but really at this point I would just love some of

02:59:54.768 --> 02:59:58.121
that budget to go towards bettering the existing parts of the city and

02:59:58.621 --> 03:00:02.247
maintaining trail connection, even if that's one of the things. So So thank you.

03:00:04.020 --> 03:00:04.394
That is all.

03:00:09.680 --> 03:00:11.160
Okay. I have a feeling there's more.

03:00:14.072 --> 03:00:18.818
Charlotte Ducos. All right.

03:00:18.915 --> 03:00:22.385
Charlotte Ducos. I actually— I love the idea

03:00:22.885 --> 03:00:26.178
of trying to improve communication. I think that having a sign

03:00:28.093 --> 03:00:31.406
Especially if it's multidirectional, so if you can see it from both

03:00:31.906 --> 03:00:34.783
sides, I think is a good idea. I do think that we need to communicate

03:00:35.283 --> 03:00:38.540
with our residents what's going on and who's invited and all of that

03:00:38.557 --> 03:00:41.952
information. I hope that the cost comes down a little bit from what's

03:00:42.033 --> 03:00:44.047
on there. I think that's a lot of money.

03:00:46.066 --> 03:00:49.540
And so I'm hoping that it is a little bit lower than that. But the

03:00:49.556 --> 03:00:52.060
thing that I actually really wanted to speak to is— Thank you.

03:00:52.547 --> 03:00:55.421
Because of the project that I do in my classroom,

03:00:56.095 --> 03:00:59.515
where we've looked at the budget for many years, I know that those utility sales

03:00:59.612 --> 03:01:02.518
funds are something that have been sitting there for a really long time because they've

03:01:03.018 --> 03:01:06.082
been there since my project started. I also know that one of

03:01:06.582 --> 03:01:09.856
the proposed uses for those funds was the city streetscapes here

03:01:09.904 --> 03:01:13.790
in City Center, as well as other

03:01:14.079 --> 03:01:17.145
types of improvements in the city center.

03:01:18.350 --> 03:01:22.012
My— what I heard tonight is that $200,000,

03:01:22.028 --> 03:01:24.967
$300,000 of that is going to go to a sign,

03:01:25.064 --> 03:01:28.324
possibly, which means that there's a whole lot more that's

03:01:28.437 --> 03:01:32.276
still there for those streetscapes. I think if we're looking at trying to build

03:01:32.308 --> 03:01:35.890
an upscale city center, we need to start working on those

03:01:36.066 --> 03:01:39.295
streetscapes. And so my encouragement is, if you want to build

03:01:39.795 --> 03:01:42.893
a sign, let's build it at the most cost-effective way that we can. I think

03:01:43.393 --> 03:01:46.839
that it has value. But I think we need to

03:01:47.339 --> 03:01:50.880
commit to doing these projects that have been sitting there for a really long time

03:01:50.993 --> 03:01:54.553
and really contribute to the quality of life and the aesthetic

03:01:55.053 --> 03:01:57.872
beauty of our city, particularly here in the city center.

03:01:58.690 --> 03:02:01.497
As well as one of the things that I heard a lot as I campaigned

03:02:01.997 --> 03:02:06.372
last year is the visibility and the landscaping and

03:02:06.549 --> 03:02:09.499
the water waste that is on ranches.

03:02:09.580 --> 03:02:13.306
Parkway as well, and we talked about

03:02:13.806 --> 03:02:16.968
the expense of xeriscaping some of those areas,

03:02:17.064 --> 03:02:20.292
taking down some of that berming and making it a little easier to see

03:02:20.792 --> 03:02:23.392
there. And it seems like some of that money might be able to be used

03:02:24.211 --> 03:02:27.728
for some of our hardest visibility areas,

03:02:29.126 --> 03:02:33.029
you know, along our main corridors as well. And so I know

03:02:33.077 --> 03:02:36.577
that some of it is not just having the funding; it's having the. people and

03:02:36.593 --> 03:02:39.961
the manpower to do the job. I understand that that is a huge issue

03:02:40.025 --> 03:02:43.168
there, but, but if the money can be used for

03:02:43.184 --> 03:02:46.985
the landscaping, it can probably be used for some manpower as well if we need

03:02:47.001 --> 03:02:50.609
to hire in some seasonal to help with that. Very second second. And so

03:02:51.010 --> 03:02:54.666
that would be my encouragement, is as we're looking at our next year's budget,

03:02:55.741 --> 03:02:59.365
as was mentioned, those, those dollars are depreciating, so let's get them spent.

03:02:59.910 --> 03:03:03.384
And I would encourage it to be on of those projects that have already

03:03:03.449 --> 03:03:07.167
been— like, we allocated it and then unallocated it. So let's reallocate that

03:03:07.588 --> 03:03:11.548
and get some of those longstanding projects done that would benefit residents

03:03:11.629 --> 03:03:12.630
in this area of town.

03:03:15.580 --> 03:03:18.524
Okay, is there anyone else who would like to speak on this topic?

03:03:20.820 --> 03:03:23.905
Sorry, I beat you to it. Okay, we'll have you go, and then,

03:03:24.100 --> 03:03:26.178
and then just make sure you fill out the paperwork and give it to Lacey

03:03:26.276 --> 03:03:29.371
afterwards, but state your name for the record. And you can turn in your paper.

03:03:29.451 --> 03:03:33.287
Go ahead. Uh, Jay Johnson. I live in Glenmar Ranches.

03:03:33.704 --> 03:03:38.134
Um, I work for Yesco. I proposed the display

03:03:38.599 --> 03:03:41.921
that, uh, you guys are looking at. Um, one of the things that was

03:03:41.970 --> 03:03:45.212
brought up was maintenance. Um, there is some

03:03:45.712 --> 03:03:48.726
maintenance to, uh, to anything, um, but it's very low,

03:03:48.743 --> 03:03:52.065
uh, a lot less than 10 or 15% on average.

03:03:52.322 --> 03:03:55.706
Um, I could get you numbers on that if people are interested.

03:03:56.669 --> 03:04:00.101
The other big thing is, is the city, I'm sure, has advertising dollars they're

03:04:00.601 --> 03:04:04.078
spending elsewhere that they could probably take that from certain

03:04:04.110 --> 03:04:06.933
areas and put into this.

03:04:07.751 --> 03:04:11.744
So the display is quite a bit larger than the other ones, and I

03:04:12.244 --> 03:04:16.010
do agree that it could be better managed as far as the content that's

03:04:16.074 --> 03:04:19.265
on it, which Yesco could help with. Thank you.

03:04:19.442 --> 03:04:23.069
as well. So, um, one big thing with the new display,

03:04:23.438 --> 03:04:27.530
um, it does come with a 5-year warranty on everything. So there's

03:04:27.546 --> 03:04:31.029
no maintenance from an initial standpoint for

03:04:31.157 --> 03:04:34.576
5 years. So, um, again, just— I

03:04:34.672 --> 03:04:37.352
live in Glenmar Ranch, is real close to this. One other thing that people will

03:04:37.400 --> 03:04:40.466
have a question about is light pollution. Um,

03:04:40.867 --> 03:04:43.900
this sign's designed to be directional for the light, um,

03:04:44.237 --> 03:04:48.163
and then it follows a lot stricter rules on where the

03:04:48.663 --> 03:04:52.055
light can broadcast and how far it does and how it dims. So I

03:04:52.168 --> 03:04:55.530
live close enough that if this was to go up tomorrow, I would see it

03:04:56.030 --> 03:04:59.395
from my back porch. So if I didn't want it there, that,

03:05:00.217 --> 03:05:03.841
that would be a thing. Or there's already lighting issues with this particular

03:05:03.921 --> 03:05:06.633
area. So anyways, thank you.

03:05:10.638 --> 03:05:14.683
Jeff Ruth. Jeff Ruth

03:05:14.715 --> 03:05:18.139
from the Landing neighborhood. I just want to say I like the idea

03:05:18.155 --> 03:05:21.658
of the sign. I think bringing out that road sign, parking it over there,

03:05:21.787 --> 03:05:24.499
it gives very little information. It's tacky.

03:05:26.312 --> 03:05:30.002
I think the sign is very much needed, especially for all the events at Corey

03:05:30.018 --> 03:05:33.510
Ride. So like I say, I think it's a good idea

03:05:34.010 --> 03:05:35.431
and money well spent. Thank you.

03:05:38.854 --> 03:05:42.445
All right, is that everybody? Seeing no one,

03:05:45.470 --> 03:05:49.518
seeing no one, we will close the public hearing portion at 8:39

03:05:50.117 --> 03:05:53.481
and bring it back to the council. I think it'd be useful

03:05:53.595 --> 03:05:57.159
just to have a quick refresher course on what

03:05:57.659 --> 03:06:01.095
the utility sale funds were like designated as and what is

03:06:01.209 --> 03:06:04.668
already committed, because I feel I feel like within the last 8

03:06:05.168 --> 03:06:10.256
years, we've talked about a very specific dollar amount for medians

03:06:10.288 --> 03:06:14.158
in city center and park strips, streetscapes,

03:06:14.286 --> 03:06:17.112
all of those things. And we've also,

03:06:17.995 --> 03:06:20.645
when the original council and mayor, which I was not on,

03:06:21.110 --> 03:06:24.306
made kind of the determination of buckets of

03:06:24.338 --> 03:06:28.143
money, they wanted to make sure that the money was evenly

03:06:28.643 --> 03:06:32.206
spread spent between the ranches and city center so that it

03:06:32.706 --> 03:06:35.770
wouldn't be all of the stuff up at the ranches. And there were

03:06:35.835 --> 03:06:39.508
some improvements made to the medians there when

03:06:39.605 --> 03:06:43.018
Mayor Pengra was our mayor to help reduce the

03:06:43.518 --> 03:06:46.981
plants that had water needs and different things like that. So it's

03:06:47.481 --> 03:06:50.862
kind of almost like a phased approach, but maybe someone from staff,

03:06:50.927 --> 03:06:54.246
either someone who's been here a long time or maybe Brad, can talk about— Yeah.

03:06:55.084 --> 03:06:58.924
Where we're headed with the funds for streetscapes. Is it

03:06:58.956 --> 03:07:02.282
still allocated or have we peeled that back?

03:07:02.378 --> 03:07:04.981
And just kind of give us an update to that because I think it's a

03:07:05.045 --> 03:07:07.777
very valuable and important question that was asked.

03:07:08.484 --> 03:07:11.697
So we do that every year in our budget when we go through the budget

03:07:12.197 --> 03:07:15.505
meetings, which one of them will be later today. And each time we look

03:07:16.005 --> 03:07:20.711
at those funds, she shows us— and last year when she showed us, we reallocated

03:07:21.211 --> 03:07:24.291
those Because like currently the medians are coming out.

03:07:24.950 --> 03:07:28.819
As opposed to improving those, we're actually gonna pull those out and expand

03:07:29.319 --> 03:07:32.801
lanes. So, but we look at those every single year in our budget process,

03:07:32.914 --> 03:07:36.575
that entire account. Agreed. I just think that some

03:07:36.607 --> 03:07:39.722
of the reasons that have been given about why we weren't gonna pull them out

03:07:40.222 --> 03:07:43.800
was one, we didn't know if medians were gonna stay. Two, there were utilities

03:07:43.832 --> 03:07:47.461
that were coming and going in the spaces. On the park

03:07:47.961 --> 03:07:51.475
strips. And instead of having to pay to landscape it twice,

03:07:51.523 --> 03:07:55.313
those were some of the things. So maybe Brad can just give some context for

03:07:55.813 --> 03:07:59.102
us. Yeah, I think you're spot on. I mean, I think that that's what

03:07:59.602 --> 03:08:02.940
we brought up back in Pangra's time. We had moved

03:08:03.440 --> 03:08:06.520
forward and we were going to do a bunch of landscaping. And then

03:08:06.536 --> 03:08:10.518
we had a whole bunch of utility companies coming through with fiber lines

03:08:10.567 --> 03:08:12.750
and all sorts of things down the side sides of roads.

03:08:13.601 --> 03:08:17.535
And we've watched that happen in the ranches where it's all developed and whatnot.

03:08:17.615 --> 03:08:20.923
And as a staff member, I didn't feel like it

03:08:21.423 --> 03:08:25.371
was adequate at the time to go in and install all this brand new landscaping

03:08:25.981 --> 03:08:29.545
just to have trenches and everything cut into it. Now I

03:08:29.594 --> 03:08:33.255
get that there is a timing for everything and that you should improve

03:08:33.271 --> 03:08:36.594
some of these things and whatnot, but that time just didn't feel like it

03:08:36.642 --> 03:08:40.400
was the time. We had heavy growth, tons of fiber lines,

03:08:40.432 --> 03:08:43.820
tons of utilities down the sides of the road.

03:08:44.045 --> 03:08:46.325
And so we've kind of held back on that.

03:08:47.079 --> 03:08:50.307
I'm speaking off— I haven't looked at the utility

03:08:50.807 --> 03:08:54.401
self-funds, so you can't hold me to the exact number, but I'm pretty sure there

03:08:54.901 --> 03:08:59.266
was around $500,000 for medians, and I think there was about $750,000

03:08:59.298 --> 03:09:02.975
for the landscapes on the sides of the road that was allocated.

03:09:03.088 --> 03:09:06.813
and then initial utility sale funds from those sale

03:09:06.845 --> 03:09:10.586
proceeds. That being said, I don't think that those have ever been

03:09:10.618 --> 03:09:14.119
grouped back into the— they got sucked back in. We've always

03:09:14.151 --> 03:09:17.972
kept them line itemed out. And so if it is something

03:09:18.004 --> 03:09:21.681
that we wanna move forward, that's something that we can discuss in the budget process.

03:09:21.778 --> 03:09:25.294
And if we wanna see it done, then we prioritize it and we figure out

03:09:25.794 --> 03:09:29.244
a plan to get it done. That's my recommendation. Thank you. I— oh,

03:09:29.324 --> 03:09:33.338
sorry. Is that right? I'm a chatty Cathy tonight. I just

03:09:33.838 --> 03:09:37.481
think that maybe being cognizant of the whole utilities

03:09:37.658 --> 03:09:41.415
thing and having to deal with the trench digging and replacing

03:09:41.431 --> 03:09:44.722
and all of those things, we are a city that

03:09:45.222 --> 03:09:48.399
can figure things out and come up with creative solutions.

03:09:48.817 --> 03:09:52.173
And maybe we look at ways that we can improve the look and feel and

03:09:52.205 --> 03:09:56.412
continuity of the city city center streetscapes

03:09:56.926 --> 03:10:00.460
without making it subject to as much change

03:10:00.476 --> 03:10:03.930
because of utilities. So, and I agree, I think that

03:10:04.430 --> 03:10:07.688
that line, those 2 line items were kept separate. At least that's the

03:10:08.188 --> 03:10:11.527
way that I remember it. So it's always been like a little bucket of money

03:10:11.639 --> 03:10:15.125
that eventually we were going to deal with it. So anyway.

03:10:15.896 --> 03:10:19.638
Brad, you and I over the years went back and forth on Sage Valley,

03:10:19.654 --> 03:10:23.353
and I would just Just imagine there's probably 20 people like that that you

03:10:23.370 --> 03:10:26.618
have calling you saying, hey, can we get a trail? Can we do this?

03:10:27.022 --> 03:10:30.841
Those little connections, they— last year, for example, we finished

03:10:31.341 --> 03:10:34.556
a path around Sage Valley, and that was such

03:10:35.056 --> 03:10:38.136
a huge win, you have no idea. That just that project, I don't know how

03:10:38.168 --> 03:10:41.153
it was paid for. Maybe it came out of this. But do you have a

03:10:41.169 --> 03:10:45.121
list of projects like that that are big

03:10:45.186 --> 03:10:48.782
wins for the residents? Yeah. But maybe not a relatively huge cost

03:10:48.895 --> 03:10:51.079
for the city or to pull from this fund?

03:10:52.428 --> 03:10:56.201
So I— no, I don't have a big list. And, and part of it

03:10:56.701 --> 03:10:59.606
is, is like, uh, over here in the city center, there is a lot of

03:11:00.106 --> 03:11:02.962
open space acres. And I mean, we've had a lot of discussions about that.

03:11:03.043 --> 03:11:06.736
And so it's— there, by volume, there's so much. And so where do

03:11:07.236 --> 03:11:10.735
you start? Where do you stop? Um, what are we

03:11:11.235 --> 03:11:12.903
going to do with it long term? Are we going to keep some of it?

03:11:12.983 --> 03:11:16.340
Are we not going to keep some of it. Like, we've had discussions about every

03:11:16.388 --> 03:11:19.906
scenario, and so I think collectively we just

03:11:20.406 --> 03:11:23.584
