that are in the bond or that are in the, what we call the

03:39:04.615 --> 03:39:08.254
indenture, which is the legal governing provisions for

03:39:08.302 --> 03:39:10.757
the bonds. And to the extent there was,

03:39:12.056 --> 03:39:15.361
per se, not sufficient what we call debt service coverage,

03:39:16.351 --> 03:39:19.687
You know, potentially the city might have to raise rates

03:39:21.289 --> 03:39:25.439
in order to meet those coverage ratios. Now, from what I understand,

03:39:25.729 --> 03:39:29.071
and I'll let Laura speak on this, the financial position of the city is such

03:39:29.167 --> 03:39:32.412
that I don't anticipate that that would have to happen in the future,

03:39:32.477 --> 03:39:35.784
but I don't want to speak for the— So yeah, Laura's raising her hand,

03:39:35.801 --> 03:39:39.262
so I'd like to make sure that she has the time because I

03:39:39.762 --> 03:39:41.886
do believe they will. They will. Okay. I misspoke.

03:39:44.700 --> 03:39:48.194
Go ahead, Laura. Thank you. As Gary indicated,

03:39:48.436 --> 03:39:52.283
uh, with the security for these bonds, there would be no obligation

03:39:52.396 --> 03:39:55.873
for the city to raise taxes. That is crystal clear under state law.

03:39:56.702 --> 03:39:59.681
If you had such an obligation, that would have to be voted upon by your

03:40:00.181 --> 03:40:03.949
citizens. So what we pledge here are those system

03:40:04.449 --> 03:40:08.315
revenues, which are inclusive of water, Sewer and storm

03:40:08.815 --> 03:40:11.794
sewer. This is a very large project,

03:40:12.020 --> 03:40:15.499
and I anticipate that the city will need to continue.

03:40:15.579 --> 03:40:19.585
It's done a very good job in the past, in recent

03:40:19.665 --> 03:40:23.413
past, of increasing your water and sewer

03:40:23.446 --> 03:40:27.350
rates occasionally. We are looking

03:40:27.399 --> 03:40:30.642
to structure the debt so that it will increase over

03:40:31.142 --> 03:40:35.209
time, unlike, you know, when you all go get a residential

03:40:35.709 --> 03:40:39.143
mortgage. Okay. I get a residential mortgage. If I want a fixed rate,

03:40:39.528 --> 03:40:42.497
I get a fixed payment that's the same every month. They don't care if I'm

03:40:42.997 --> 03:40:46.365
going to be earning more or less in the future. I just get the same

03:40:46.865 --> 03:40:50.137
payment. In the municipal bond world, we have the ability

03:40:50.637 --> 03:40:53.941
to structure the debt around estimated

03:40:55.337 --> 03:40:58.884
increases in revenue. So when we think of Eagle Mountain,

03:40:58.900 --> 03:41:02.847
it's like, oh, there's going to be a lot of population. growth. So we are

03:41:02.879 --> 03:41:06.311
working with a debt service coverage model

03:41:06.744 --> 03:41:10.240
where we'll take into account what your historical population growth has

03:41:10.336 --> 03:41:14.009
been. We'll probably dial that down again to show the markets that

03:41:14.025 --> 03:41:18.034
we're being, you know, conservative. But in addition to that,

03:41:18.114 --> 03:41:21.418
I believe there will need to be rate increases of—

03:41:21.918 --> 03:41:24.609
I mean, I'm ballparking it now, so please don't, you know,

03:41:25.010 --> 03:41:28.522
freak out or whatever, because we still have a lot of number crunching

03:41:28.554 --> 03:41:32.291
to do, but I'm expecting, you know, somewhere between,

03:41:32.307 --> 03:41:36.092
you know, 3% and maybe 5% on,

03:41:36.156 --> 03:41:40.021
you know, a multi-annual basis.

03:41:40.566 --> 03:41:43.068
Again, it depends on, on the population.

03:41:43.805 --> 03:41:47.285
So when we issue the bonds, as Gary indicated, you will

03:41:47.478 --> 03:41:51.535
covenant that you will maintain rates and charges such

03:41:51.648 --> 03:41:56.009
that your net revenues So all your gross revenues less

03:41:56.057 --> 03:42:00.558
your operating expenses will generate $1.25

03:42:00.816 --> 03:42:03.963
for every dollar in debt payment. We'll be very mindful

03:42:04.463 --> 03:42:07.985
of that as we're issuing the bonds and we show them a projected

03:42:08.017 --> 03:42:11.157
debt service coverage table. So that is what

03:42:11.190 --> 03:42:14.725
we're expecting at the moment. Again, we'll refine that as we move forward.

03:42:17.801 --> 03:42:20.933
Well said, Laura. Yeah, legally there's not a requirement other

03:42:21.433 --> 03:42:24.480
than to We're going to meet the ratios, but she's right, you know, practically speaking,

03:42:24.836 --> 03:42:28.196
it sounds like there may have to be some raising

03:42:28.244 --> 03:42:29.779
of the rates over time.

03:42:34.948 --> 03:42:36.876
Okay. I don't see any other questions.

03:42:42.185 --> 03:42:45.246
If there's nothing else to speak on, I'll entertain a motion.

03:42:49.680 --> 03:42:52.997
Nobody wants to make that motion. Okay,

03:42:53.285 --> 03:42:56.955
Council Member Whitey. Okay, Mayor, I motion—

03:42:57.067 --> 03:43:00.753
I move to approve a resolution of Eagle Mountain

03:43:01.253 --> 03:43:04.871
City, Utah, authorizing the issuance and sale of not more than—

03:43:04.887 --> 03:43:08.909
we're saying $230 million aggregate

03:43:09.409 --> 03:43:12.563
principal amount of water and sewer revenue bonds Series 2026.

03:43:14.029 --> 03:43:16.178
As presented here in the agenda.

03:43:17.939 --> 03:43:21.379
Second. Okay, we have a motion by Councilmember

03:43:21.879 --> 03:43:24.402
Whiting and a second by Councilmember Wright. Is there any questions or discussion on the

03:43:24.902 --> 03:43:27.555
motion? Can I just make a statement, a public statement? Sure.

03:43:28.492 --> 03:43:32.551
I think it's just important for our residents to understand. I mean, $230 million makes

03:43:33.051 --> 03:43:34.841
me sick to my stomach. I'm just gonna say that.

03:43:36.831 --> 03:43:40.607
Kind of feels like when I bought my first mortgage. I left

03:43:41.107 --> 03:43:43.651
there excited and happy, and I'm not even excited and happy about this.

03:43:44.914 --> 03:43:48.022
But this is an absolute necessity for our city, and I hope that

03:43:48.135 --> 03:43:52.785
our citizens will recognize and understand that

03:43:52.801 --> 03:43:56.802
the necessity of this has been coming our way for a number of years,

03:43:56.995 --> 03:44:00.221
and we have not paid the attention to it that perhaps we should

03:44:00.721 --> 03:44:04.416
have. When we received some of our earlier information

03:44:04.916 --> 03:44:09.675
that our loading is at 100% in our current plant, the 2.4 million gallon plant.

03:44:10.352 --> 03:44:14.268
The loading is the capacity of the plant to produce or to process

03:44:14.381 --> 03:44:16.493
the sludge. I'm gonna call it sludge. I don't know what they call it,

03:44:16.509 --> 03:44:20.414
chocolate milk, I'm not sure. But to process that,

03:44:20.430 --> 03:44:23.296
the heavy solids in the sewer treatment plant,

03:44:23.393 --> 03:44:27.644
we really thought we had still another 3 or 4 year window that we could

03:44:27.821 --> 03:44:31.493
work towards this outcome. When that came to us

03:44:31.993 --> 03:44:34.406
earlier this year, and we've been working on this for a couple of years.

03:44:34.454 --> 03:44:38.211
This isn't brand new. But we're really at a point of no return

03:44:38.259 --> 03:44:41.707
where if we do nothing, you're going to

03:44:41.723 --> 03:44:45.437
all recognize it. And I would rather deal with you being frustrated

03:44:45.937 --> 03:44:49.248
by a bond issuance than by the alternative that that

03:44:49.748 --> 03:44:53.117
might create in your individual home. And there's

03:44:53.617 --> 03:44:57.383
been promises made by this body and others to the development

03:44:57.883 --> 03:45:02.054
community and to those who are building in our city currently that

03:45:02.554 --> 03:45:05.675
we'll be able to process that waste and

03:45:06.175 --> 03:45:08.313
that we'll be able to get water to their homes so that they can have

03:45:08.410 --> 03:45:11.731
drinking tap water just like you do. And without

03:45:11.860 --> 03:45:15.144
this, we're not going to be able to meet those obligations,

03:45:15.176 --> 03:45:18.879
and that's not a place that you want us to put you either. So that's

03:45:19.379 --> 03:45:22.585
a really simple explanation. I'll be happy to go into detail with anybody that wants

03:45:23.085 --> 03:45:26.458
to hear it. Give me a call, send me an email, let's talk about

03:45:26.958 --> 03:45:29.803
it. But the reality of it is, is This is the responsible thing for us

03:45:30.303 --> 03:45:33.978
to do regarding our fiduciary duty. And I'll be quiet and let the mayor call

03:45:34.478 --> 03:45:37.896
for the vote now. Craig also wants to make a comment. Yeah, and this might

03:45:38.396 --> 03:45:40.320
be a question better answered by Ben or someone else,

03:45:40.385 --> 03:45:44.046
but another— I think questions

03:45:44.062 --> 03:45:47.674
that citizens might have regarding this is, wouldn't impact

03:45:48.174 --> 03:45:51.961
fees typically be enough to not get us in a $200 million hole? And why—

03:45:52.461 --> 03:45:55.460
so why are we here? I think it would be helpful to answer that question

03:45:55.960 --> 03:45:59.100
as well. Sure. So first of all, the reason you can't pledge

03:45:59.228 --> 03:46:02.741
impact fees is because they come in inconsistently. So you don't—

03:46:02.917 --> 03:46:06.606
there's no guarantee that there's going to be 1,000 new homes every single year to

03:46:07.106 --> 03:46:09.845
be able to pay those. There might be a year where you have 2,000.

03:46:09.893 --> 03:46:14.608
There might be a year where we have 2008-09

03:46:15.041 --> 03:46:18.537
where the housing market drops off. And so we have

03:46:18.553 --> 03:46:22.406
to use collateral from a revenue standpoint from the actual user

03:46:22.486 --> 03:46:26.244
rates. So that's probably one answer. But the second,

03:46:26.597 --> 03:46:30.145
I think, more philosophical answer to this question is that we

03:46:30.210 --> 03:46:34.127
are building a facility that is a 50-year or longer

03:46:34.627 --> 03:46:38.928
facility. And the benefit to those

03:46:39.362 --> 03:46:42.091
not only comes to the existing residents that live here today,

03:46:42.573 --> 03:46:46.796
but the benefit will come to those residents that move

03:46:47.296 --> 03:46:48.594
in and live here and raise their families or their kids or grandkids into the

03:46:49.094 --> 03:46:53.138
future as our population continues to grow. And so proportionately speaking,

03:46:53.331 --> 03:46:57.345
it makes more sense for us to ensure that impact fees and

03:46:57.845 --> 03:47:01.504
user rates over time are paid for, for an asset that's

03:47:02.004 --> 03:47:05.374
used over time as well, so that those who are reaping the

03:47:05.454 --> 03:47:08.537
benefit of this asset are helping to contribute and pay for it,

03:47:08.681 --> 03:47:12.519
and it's not all on the backs of existing residents. And historic

03:47:13.019 --> 03:47:16.228
impact fees, to explain, it's not like the

03:47:16.728 --> 03:47:19.889
city could have charged more historic impact fees to help us not be

03:47:20.389 --> 03:47:23.485
in this situation, is that correct? That is correct. So historic impact fees,

03:47:23.501 --> 03:47:27.419
of course, have been used to pay for only what's in our capital facility plan,

03:47:27.612 --> 03:47:31.096
more specifically in our impact fee facility plan

03:47:31.225 --> 03:47:34.420
and our impact fee analysis, which is

03:47:34.516 --> 03:47:37.728
only over a 6-year project or period. So only those projects,

03:47:37.792 --> 03:47:41.726
what you do over a 6-year, that 6-year window, and that's pursuant

03:47:42.226 --> 03:47:45.611
to state code. And so we, we could only do projects that we

03:47:45.916 --> 03:47:49.609
already had in there— trunk lines, lift stations, things like

03:47:50.109 --> 03:47:53.447
that. And while we anticipated doing upgrades to the plant,

03:47:53.463 --> 03:47:57.060
we, and we have collected money for that, those upgrades have

03:47:57.560 --> 03:48:00.865
been done. At this stage, we need to be able to make

03:48:01.365 --> 03:48:04.030
significant increases to our overall plant capacity.

03:48:04.689 --> 03:48:08.545
And the way you can ensure that future impact fees can be used

03:48:09.045 --> 03:48:12.643
on that is by incorporating the debt service into the IFA so that that

03:48:13.143 --> 03:48:16.917
debt service over the next 30 years is— or 35 years or whatever it

03:48:17.417 --> 03:48:20.934
ends up being— is being paid towards that debt as those new

03:48:20.999 --> 03:48:25.112
residents are moving in. So new growth should be paying for new— That

03:48:25.128 --> 03:48:29.030
makes sense. And just to hopefully Hopefully that the citizens heard portion of

03:48:29.530 --> 03:48:33.536
what you said is state law dictates how much a

03:48:34.036 --> 03:48:37.321
city can collect in impact fees. The city's been following the state law in terms

03:48:37.821 --> 03:48:41.202
of what impact fees have been collected, so it's not like the city wasn't following

03:48:41.234 --> 03:48:44.891
that and suddenly because we weren't following that now we have this bond that

03:48:45.391 --> 03:48:47.745
has to be issued. We followed what the state has allowed us to do,

03:48:48.675 --> 03:48:52.284
and we are now at this juncture where we're faced with what

03:48:52.784 --> 03:48:56.890
is now. You know, there's something else that maybe,

03:48:58.910 --> 03:49:03.112
having been through this before in a neighboring city and

03:49:03.192 --> 03:49:06.235
having seen the pain and struggles and everything,

03:49:06.735 --> 03:49:10.327
there's one element of this entire project that's really not ever talked

03:49:10.827 --> 03:49:13.987
about. And that's the fact that we're using what's called membrane biotechnology,

03:49:14.804 --> 03:49:18.213
bioreactor technology, an MBR. This is going to create a high-quality

03:49:18.261 --> 03:49:21.505
wastewater. Yeah. Quality Type 1 sewer effluent that can be

03:49:21.521 --> 03:49:24.572
used and reused. Now, we're working with the state of Utah right

03:49:25.072 --> 03:49:29.309
now, amongst other cities, trying to be able to reuse that water resource within our

03:49:29.373 --> 03:49:33.403
community, and it will be a perpetual, ever-growing resource, which is fantastic

03:49:33.903 --> 03:49:36.438
for our, you know, the 2nd most arid state in the nation.

03:49:37.096 --> 03:49:41.255
So that is a fantastic thing. But what is the actual economic

03:49:41.755 --> 03:49:46.200
value if we were to try to go out and buy 6 million

03:49:46.700 --> 03:49:48.799
gallons per day of water off the open market. That is what we're producing.

03:49:48.847 --> 03:49:52.905
So there's an element of what we can do with that

03:49:53.163 --> 03:49:57.128
should the state approve the reuse application that we have in right now. So there

03:49:57.177 --> 03:49:58.435
is an asset to this as well.

03:50:01.170 --> 03:50:04.445
Melissa, I let everybody else speak, so before we do

03:50:04.945 --> 03:50:08.304
a vote, happy to— Sure, I'm happy to. I think the reality of it is

03:50:10.516 --> 03:50:14.138
Government exists for the very basics— fire,

03:50:14.699 --> 03:50:19.074
police, water, sewer, roads— and

03:50:19.827 --> 03:50:23.432
we've tackled a bunch of those things tonight.

03:50:23.673 --> 03:50:27.086
But the reality of it is, is this is a test we can't afford to

03:50:27.679 --> 03:50:31.461
not get right. I've had neighbors who've had sewage

03:50:31.509 --> 03:50:35.595
backup in their house because of some local issue in their neighborhood.

03:50:36.479 --> 03:50:40.961
And it sucks really bad, and I can't imagine how awful

03:50:40.994 --> 03:50:44.432
we would be as public servants if we let that happen. And the

03:50:44.932 --> 03:50:47.822
reality of it is, is this is just like big boy and big girl stuff.

03:50:48.288 --> 03:50:51.582
This is what we got to do, and we're—we were elected to make these tough

03:50:52.082 --> 03:50:55.390
decisions. And I think the important part is this

03:50:55.519 --> 03:50:59.069
is a very open and public process so that our residents

03:50:59.569 --> 03:51:01.865
can be a part of it. We want you guys to come and tell.

03:51:01.930 --> 03:51:06.021
Tell us what you think and weigh in on it and help

03:51:06.086 --> 03:51:10.308
us get to the right solution together. So I echo everything

03:51:10.372 --> 03:51:13.575
that's been asked and said, but that's my 2 cents on it.

03:51:15.310 --> 03:51:18.855
Yeah, and I'll just say this, Councilmember Wright. In your first couple months of service,

03:51:18.920 --> 03:51:21.376
we approved a big bond that made your stomach hurt.

03:51:22.302 --> 03:51:25.688
And now, now we're doing another one in your first few months of your second

03:51:26.188 --> 03:51:28.214
term. You're not running for a third term, are you? No chance of that.

03:51:28.310 --> 03:51:31.393
It's going to get even bigger. I'm just teasing.

03:51:32.212 --> 03:51:35.551
All right, we'll go ahead. Motion's still on the table,

03:51:36.225 --> 03:51:39.533
and Councilmember Hewish is going to be recused.

03:51:40.191 --> 03:51:43.964
Councilmember Clark? Yes. Councilmember Wright?

03:51:44.044 --> 03:51:46.837
Yes. Councilmember Whiting? Yes.

03:51:48.218 --> 03:51:50.578
Okay, that passes. I'm glad we got through that one.

03:51:52.055 --> 03:51:55.019
The next one, ordinance this is a public hearing.

03:51:55.571 --> 03:51:59.625
Thank you, Laura. Yes, and I think it was Greg,

03:51:59.641 --> 03:52:01.694
right? Garrett. Thank you, Garrett.

03:52:04.870 --> 03:52:08.306
So the next— an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City, Utah, amending the Municipal Code

03:52:08.354 --> 03:52:10.952
1595 and adopting the,

03:52:12.000 --> 03:52:15.953
the Eagle Mountain City Wildland Urban Interface Area Map, known to—

03:52:16.018 --> 03:52:19.373
known at the state as WUI. And we talk a lot about

03:52:23.289 --> 03:52:26.727
I've got a 30-minute presentation for you, but I don't want to give that to

03:52:27.227 --> 03:52:29.250
you. Condense it.

03:52:30.487 --> 03:52:32.954
I'm just kidding. I know I'm not going to give you that.

03:52:35.396 --> 03:52:38.583
So the Wildland Urban Interface,

03:52:39.098 --> 03:52:44.492
WUI, is an area that—

03:52:44.992 --> 03:52:49.292
well, first of all, there is a WUI code. Wildland Urban Interface Code.

03:52:49.356 --> 03:52:53.091
The state has the State Construction

03:52:53.123 --> 03:52:56.585
Code. It's in 15A, Title 15A of the

03:52:57.085 --> 03:53:01.506
state code. And that State Construction Code adopted

03:53:01.923 --> 03:53:05.546
the 2006 Wildland Urban

03:53:06.046 --> 03:53:09.585
Interface Code. And then to kind of connect the dots a little bit here,

03:53:10.259 --> 03:53:13.978
in Chapter 1595 of our code, we've adopted the

03:53:14.026 --> 03:53:17.944
state construction code pretty much in its entirety. That includes

03:53:17.976 --> 03:53:21.412
the WUI code. So what's interesting, if you look in our code,

03:53:22.311 --> 03:53:24.800
we've adopted it, but we don't say where it's applicable.

03:53:26.229 --> 03:53:30.082
On top of that, we are part of what's called the

03:53:30.582 --> 03:53:34.402
Cooperative Wildfire System. And the

03:53:34.434 --> 03:53:37.051
wildfire system is basically,

03:53:38.030 --> 03:53:41.671
it's run by the Division of Forestry, Fire, and State

03:53:42.171 --> 03:53:45.851
Lands. It's, the simple principle

03:53:46.351 --> 03:53:50.316
basically is, okay, state will pay for our wildland

03:53:50.364 --> 03:53:54.289
fire, fighting wildland fires, as long

03:53:54.321 --> 03:53:59.311
as we work to prevent

03:53:59.635 --> 03:54:03.505
and mitigate some of the fire hazards in our jurisdiction.

03:54:03.683 --> 03:54:08.053
So, House Bill 48

03:54:08.117 --> 03:54:11.366
of 2025 require— uh,

03:54:11.816 --> 03:54:15.500
requires cities that are connected to

03:54:16.000 --> 03:54:19.404
that CWS, the Cooperative Wildfire System, to enforce

03:54:19.904 --> 03:54:22.857
the WUI code. So we, we have it,

03:54:22.954 --> 03:54:26.187
we've adopted it, but we don't really enforce it because we don't have a map

03:54:26.687 --> 03:54:30.370
saying where it's applicable. So that's, that's what we're proposing,

03:54:30.872 --> 03:54:34.526
uh, That's what we've got for you tonight is a proposed map to show

03:54:34.607 --> 03:54:38.417
where the WUI code would be applicable. And so to

03:54:38.917 --> 03:54:42.453
give you a couple of examples of WUI requirements

03:54:42.602 --> 03:54:50.039
here on—

03:54:50.539 --> 03:54:54.640
so a permit is required for these types of uses like

03:54:55.337 --> 03:54:59.090
fireworks, fireworks stand, automobile wrecking yard,

03:54:59.106 --> 03:55:02.500
lumberyard, Things that are potentially flammable,

03:55:03.000 --> 03:55:06.920
you gotta get a permit through

03:55:07.017 --> 03:55:10.417
the code official, who'd be someone like the building official

03:55:10.917 --> 03:55:14.696
or maybe even our fire marshal. Dan DeVogue

03:55:15.065 --> 03:55:19.568
kind of plays that role for us. The other thing that

03:55:20.068 --> 03:55:24.878
you'll notice is there's requirements for roofing

03:55:24.975 --> 03:55:27.186
covers, eaves, downspouts.

03:55:28.599 --> 03:55:34.010
Exterior walls, you have to have higher-grade ignition-resistant

03:55:34.333 --> 03:55:36.254
materials in those areas.

03:55:37.886 --> 03:55:41.566
Other thing that's kind of probably something a number of you have heard,

03:55:41.615 --> 03:55:45.038
that a number of the council and public

03:55:45.538 --> 03:55:49.231
here may have heard of, is defensible spaces. So if you're

03:55:49.327 --> 03:55:52.429
building in the WUI, and if

03:55:52.462 --> 03:55:56.245
say you're in an extreme hazard area, You'd have to have

03:55:56.750 --> 03:56:00.094
kind of clear the brush 100 feet around a structure.

03:56:01.304 --> 03:56:04.830
